# absolute-url.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M129): a step whose target is written as an absolute URL
# rather than a path under the active env's base — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF057 **and** TF058
# (checker, tflw M125b1 / review `FU-18`, D245/D246).
#
# **One file, two codes, and which one you get is a property of the config rather than of this
# text.** That is the entire reason the fixture is shaped like `missing-base-url.tflw` next door:
# `verify-check-diagnostics.mjs` copies it into a scratch directory and checks it twice, under one
# config that declares `allow hosts` and one that declares none. Same unmodified file, two
# different diagnostics — the demonstration that the rule reads the config.
#
#   allowlist declared     → TF057   "this step is fixed where it points; `--env` will not move it"
#   no allowlist declared  → TF058   "the run will refuse to send it"
#
# **The absence of an allowlist meaning *enforcement* is the one inversion in the language**, and it
# is why these two codes exist as a pair. `allow hosts` is opt-in and unset means every host is
# permitted, which is the right default for a suite written entirely against its env's base URL —
# that base *is* the declaration of where it talks. An absolute URL is the one form that can reach a
# host `tflw.config` never mentions, so writing one opts the suite into declaring where it may
# reach. A suite with no allowlist and an absolute URL is therefore not permissive; it is a run that
# will refuse to send.
#
# **Both are warnings, and both are predictions** (D147): the operand comes from `tflw.config` and
# differs per env, so a suite whose CI env declares an allowlist is correct and an error would make
# it unrunnable with no override. TF058's *runtime* half is a refusal rather than a warning, because
# by then the config is resolved and the URL is in hand — an observation, not a prediction.
#
# **Not phrased as a mistake, because it frequently is not one.** A one-off request to a second host
# is the case `FU-18` was filed about. The warning exists so that "this step ignores the env" is
# something the file says out loud rather than something a reader has to notice.
#
# **Not covered:** an absolute URL naming a host the allowlist *excludes*. That is a refusal at run
# time with its own message, not a static fact — the checker knows the allowlist but not what the
# author meant by writing past it.
#
# Checking this file against this project's own config demonstrates TF057 only, since `env local`
# declares `allow hosts`. Run the proof that supplies both operands (also the `check-diagnostics`
# phase of `npm run regression`):
#   node scripts/verify-check-diagnostics.mjs

test "an absolute api target"
  api GET https://api.example.com/orders

test "an absolute browser target — the form that used to be silently concatenated"
  open "https://example.com/checkout"

test "the relative neighbours the rule must not touch"
  # Resolved against the env's base, which is what makes `--env staging` mean anything.
  api GET /health
  open "/"
